WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our next guest is
a Tony winning actor

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:05.071
you know from shows such as
"Masters of Sex"

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:07.006
and "Impeachment:
American Crime Story."

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:08.674
She stars in "B Positive,"

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:11.477
which airs two new back-to-back
episodes this Thursday,

00:00:11.477 --> 00:00:13.746
starting at 9:00 p.m. on CBS
and Paramount+.

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:15.448
Let's take a look.
-While I have you all,

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:16.916
let's make a mental note

00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:19.219
to check the smoke alarms
next week.

00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:22.755
It's battery changing season.
What? What?

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:24.958
-Gina, we don't usually talk
work here.

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:29.762
-Oh, right. Right. Sorry.
Yeah, we're off the clock.

00:00:29.762 --> 00:00:31.064
Hey, what are you
doing this weekend?

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:32.398
-Oh, I'm actually
taking Monday off.

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:34.033
I'm going on a hiking trip
with some friends.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.334
-Oh, that sounds great.

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:37.069
Just remember to put
in your time card tomorrow

00:00:37.069 --> 00:00:38.437
because they're due on Friday.

00:00:38.437 --> 00:00:40.539
Now, Gabby, you sent
that e-mail to everyone, right?

00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:42.908
-I could check, but I sent it
from my work computer

00:00:42.908 --> 00:00:44.510
and we're not at work.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:46.345
-Starting to feel like it.

00:00:46.345 --> 00:00:47.680
-Please welcome back to the
show,

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.249
our friend Annaleigh Ashford.

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:51.217
How are you, Annaleigh?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.951
-Hello!

00:00:52.951 --> 00:00:54.520
Aww, it's so good to see.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.389
-It's so good to see you
as well.

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:59.625
I do wish you were
here and -- yeah, there you go.

00:00:59.625 --> 00:01:01.995
-Oh, God, what a warm hug.

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:04.897
-You -- So, doing this sitcom,
I imagine one of the draws,

00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:06.399
being a stage actor
like you are,

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:07.900
is getting to do a show

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.602
in front of
the live studio audience.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:12.538
But because of COVID,
a lot of the episodes you taped

00:01:12.538 --> 00:01:13.506
were just in front of the crew.

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:16.476
How was that?

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:19.645
-I give the crew
baked goods and alcohol.

00:01:19.645 --> 00:01:21.347
I'm just...
[ Laughs ]

00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:22.815
I wish.

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:25.518
You know, thank God.
They're a wonderful audience.

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:28.288
And, hey, if the crew laughs --
You know.

00:01:28.288 --> 00:01:30.256
If the crew laughs,
then you know it went well.

00:01:30.256 --> 00:01:32.491
-Yes.
-But sometimes I feel

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:37.163
like I'm doing like
an eternal Wednesday matinee,

00:01:37.163 --> 00:01:40.032
like, the Wednesday matinee
that won't end.

00:01:40.032 --> 00:01:41.834
But that's -- hey, I'm so happy

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:44.370
to be making somebody laugh
somewhere.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.539
You just hope that
it goes through the ether

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:49.141
in the land of television,
and I imagine the laughs.

00:01:49.141 --> 00:01:51.978
But I do remember on stage

00:01:51.978 --> 00:01:54.246
having one particular
Wednesday matinee

00:01:54.246 --> 00:01:57.584
where it was so quiet

00:01:57.584 --> 00:02:00.353
that it felt like
nobody was out there.

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:02.421
And when we looked
in the front row,

00:02:02.421 --> 00:02:04.923
there was one woman
who was taking a full-out nap.

00:02:04.923 --> 00:02:07.393
And it was
it was a three-act play,

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:10.697
so it was a long play
and she took a long nap.

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:14.200
And then, you know, you start
to wonder if she was alive.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.169
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.436
She woke up at the end.

00:02:17.436 --> 00:02:19.005
The applause at the end
of the show woke her up,

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:21.140
but, you know, it sort of
feels like that sometimes.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.110
-That's very -- that's very rude
for everybody to clap

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:25.511
and wake up that poor woman.

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:28.181
-I mean, hello.

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:29.948
She was having a lovely respite.

00:02:29.948 --> 00:02:32.252
-So a three-act play,

00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:35.621
those intermissions
must be so weird with you

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:38.857
and the other actors when
you realize how quiet it is.

00:02:38.857 --> 00:02:41.961
Do you talk about it backstage?
Is there like a real like...?

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:43.396
-Immediately.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:45.097
Like, the first thing you do
when you got offstage

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:46.632
when you're doing a show is go,

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:47.867
"What's happening
with this audience?"

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:49.201
whether they're good or bad.

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:51.470
You know, they're like --
I only wish the audience knew

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:53.306
how much we talked about them.

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:56.009
And there's like, you know,
a thousand of them,

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:58.344
but they are one in our minds.
-Yeah.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.147
You know how sometimes there's
a Q&amp;A after a show

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:02.448
where the audience
can ask the cast --

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.583
you should do a Q&amp;A
where you, the cast,

00:03:04.583 --> 00:03:06.919
get to ask the audience
some questions like,

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:10.323
"What did you think
you were buying a ticket for?"

00:03:10.323 --> 00:03:12.524
-Why did you take melatonin
before coming?

00:03:12.524 --> 00:03:14.927
Could you have waited?

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:18.997
-So, your character in the show
owns and runs a nursing home.

00:03:18.997 --> 00:03:20.900
As a young performer,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.836
you actually found your way
to performing at nursing homes.

00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:26.706
How did that happen, and did you
enjoy that experience?

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:29.008
-You know,
it's such a strange story

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:32.712
that I, like, forgot happened
until a couple weeks

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:33.612
into working out of the show.

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:34.880
I was like, "Oh, you know what?

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.750
I feel like I've been here
before," because I have.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.821
When I was a kid, I danced
at this amazing dance studio,

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:44.557
and to practice numbers
before dance competitions,

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:46.692
we would play nursing homes.

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:49.228
We would do like 30 dance
numbers at these nursing homes.

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:51.831
And sometimes they were, like,
very risque.

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:54.700
Like, I remember the older girls
did a Janet Jackson number

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:58.537
and they wore, like, bustiers
and, like, ripped-up jeans

00:03:58.537 --> 00:04:00.039
and like door frames.

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:03.509
And it was -- it was a big hit
at the nursing home.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:05.078
And I would sing.

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:08.648
And I remember at the end
of one of our gigs,

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:11.150
the director of the nursing home
came up and asked me

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.552
if I wanted to do my own set.

00:04:13.552 --> 00:04:16.288
So I came back
with my karaoke machine,

00:04:16.288 --> 00:04:19.892
and I had a little mic
attached to a boombox,

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:22.161
and I had about 15 songs

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:24.397
and little karaoke tapes
in order,

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:27.433
and I wrote my own jokes,
and I wore a tuxedo.

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:28.767
-[ Chuckles ]

00:04:28.767 --> 00:04:32.205
-Why? Why did that happen?
It was very uncomfortable.

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:35.274
-They must have loved you,
right?

00:04:35.274 --> 00:04:37.376
-They love to be,
but they were like,

00:04:37.376 --> 00:04:41.280
"Why is this small woman
doing this show for us?"

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.317
And I was 10.
-But now you have --

00:04:45.317 --> 00:04:48.420
Is it true that you have always
befriended people

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.025
that were maybe a generation
or two above your age group?

00:04:52.025 --> 00:04:54.794
-Yes. You know, I've always
thought it's sort of obnoxious

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:57.763
when people are like,
"I'm an old soul. Oh."

00:04:57.763 --> 00:04:59.065
But I, you know...

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:01.533
I'm sort of -- I've always
connected with people

00:05:01.533 --> 00:05:03.970
who are of an older generation
than I am.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:07.006
And I -- You know, I'm working
with a bunch of legends

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:08.507
with a capital "L" right now.

00:05:08.507 --> 00:05:10.676
Any time Linda Lavin
will tell me any story,

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:12.912
I sit down at her feet.

00:05:12.912 --> 00:05:14.680
But, yeah,
we used to live next door

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.916
actually to my great-grandma
when I was growing up.

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:20.786
So my cousin Tyler and I
would walk home from school

00:05:20.786 --> 00:05:22.622
and we'd go next door

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:24.222
and hang out
with Grandma Ashford

00:05:24.222 --> 00:05:25.958
for a couple of hours.

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:29.394
And she -- We were in charge
of giving her her medicine,

00:05:29.394 --> 00:05:31.930
and she somehow
would make a game

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:33.298
out of throwing it
across the room

00:05:33.298 --> 00:05:35.034
'cause she just really
didn't want to take it.

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:38.837
And so we were sort of in charge
of, like, finding her pills.

00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:41.841
We'd find -- Over the years,
we found them in the couch.

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:43.475
We found them under glasses.

00:05:43.475 --> 00:05:44.810
They were in the plants.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:47.579
I really was like, "Why are we
making her take this?"

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:49.115
You know, it's probably like
extra magnesium.

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:50.416
Like, what's the point?

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:52.852
-[ Laughs ] You have --

00:05:52.852 --> 00:05:55.088
We've talked before
about your son, Jack.

00:05:55.088 --> 00:05:56.155
I think the last time
you were here

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:57.756
he was maybe only one
or two years old.

00:05:57.756 --> 00:05:59.425
He's five and a half now.
-Oh, my God.

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:00.759
-How is he doing?
-Yes, I was still --

00:06:00.759 --> 00:06:03.696
I no longer give him milk
from my breasts.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:04.730
That's what was happening
last time I was here.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.665
-We've crossed
the milk threshold.

00:06:06.665 --> 00:06:08.033
Congratulations.
-Yes.

00:06:08.033 --> 00:06:10.970
Yeah, we're done doing that.
Thank God.

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:15.541
But, yeah, he's doing great.
He's so big now, which is crazy.

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.577
He's really, really, really
into superheroes.

00:06:18.577 --> 00:06:22.148
He's super into Spider-Man
right now specifically.

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:24.450
-Does he know you are
in show business?

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.352
Does he know his mom
is an actor?

00:06:26.352 --> 00:06:30.623
-It's just started happening,
which is so banan--

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:32.024
I mean, you know,

00:06:32.024 --> 00:06:33.859
it's like such a funny thing
being in the business

00:06:33.859 --> 00:06:37.396
and then growing up,
not in the business, you know?

00:06:37.396 --> 00:06:40.432
I can't imagine what it feels
like to have a kid who just like

00:06:40.432 --> 00:06:43.268
has parents who are friends
with actors that they see

00:06:43.268 --> 00:06:45.037
in things, you know?
He'll be like, "Mom, do you --"

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:46.839
Now he's started being like,
"Do you know them?"

00:06:46.839 --> 00:06:48.273
in every movie that we watch.

00:06:48.273 --> 00:06:49.508
You know, and I don't.

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:51.777
But now,

00:06:51.777 --> 00:06:55.013
when I fight with my husband
or a couple of days ago,

00:06:55.013 --> 00:06:56.415
I got really upset
about something,

00:06:56.415 --> 00:06:58.484
I was like, "I don't understand
what's happening!"

00:06:58.484 --> 00:07:00.719
And he was like,
"Mom, are you crying,

00:07:00.719 --> 00:07:02.254
or are you working on a scene?"

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:03.723
That's literally what he said.

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:06.792
He'll go, "Are you running
lines?" when we fight.

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:11.497
And we just go, "Yes, we've got
a big scene coming up."

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:15.468
-"Your father and I
are rehearsing."

00:07:15.468 --> 00:07:18.371
-"Oh, we're doing 'Who's Afraid
of Virginia Woolf' again."

00:07:18.371 --> 00:07:20.572
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:20.572 --> 00:07:23.810
I was lucky enough to see you
on stage

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.078
doing the great
Stephen Sondheim musical

00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:27.680
"Sunday in the Park
with George."

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.248
I can only imagine,
like all of us,

00:07:30.248 --> 00:07:32.685
that you were deeply affected
by his passing.

00:07:32.685 --> 00:07:36.221
What was your -- do you have
a fond memory with Stephen

00:07:36.221 --> 00:07:38.024
you'd be willing to share?

00:07:38.024 --> 00:07:39.958
-Absolutely.
I mean, I have so many,

00:07:39.958 --> 00:07:43.896
but one thing that keeps
coming back to me

00:07:43.896 --> 00:07:49.001
is how, as an artist,
he was never done.

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:51.337
You know, one of the most famous
lines

00:07:51.337 --> 00:07:52.505
in "Sunday in the Park
with George" is

00:07:52.505 --> 00:07:53.705
"Anything you do,
let it come from you,

00:07:53.705 --> 00:07:55.040
then it will be new.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:56.909
Give us more to see."

00:07:56.909 --> 00:08:01.147
And, you know, for all of us
who want to make art,

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:02.448
that should sort of be a mantra.

00:08:02.448 --> 00:08:06.118
And it was for him,
and the art was never done.

00:08:06.118 --> 00:08:07.720
You know,
he was always giving us notes,

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.388
always giving himself notes.

00:08:09.388 --> 00:08:12.091
And I always think
it's sort of magical.

00:08:12.091 --> 00:08:14.160
He loved puzzles.
He loved board games.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.130
And on opening night,
he gave us all a puzzle.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.965
And so I just feel
like he left us

00:08:19.965 --> 00:08:23.169
all of these beautiful puzzles
to keep figuring out.

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:24.370
You know,
that's sort of his legacy.

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:27.740
These shows are like
exquisite works of art

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:32.411
that we can keep working out,
you know?

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:34.580
-It is an incredible gift
that he left us

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.748
with all that work
that we can continue to enjoy,

00:08:36.748 --> 00:08:38.684
and it is always so wonderful
to talk to you.

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:40.319
Thanks so much
for making the time, Annaleigh.

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:42.688
-Oh, my pleasure.
Good to see you.

00:08:42.688 --> 00:08:45.124
Bye!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:45.124 --> 00:08:46.725
-Annaleigh Ashford, everybody!

00:08:46.725 --> 00:08:48.694
Two new back-to-back episodes

00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:50.062
of "B Positive"
air this Thursday,

00:08:50.062 --> 00:08:52.231
starting at 9:00 p.m.
on CBS and Paramount+.

